  WHAT THIS REPORT EVIDENCES
    Complaint creation at the web tier, which makes this the baseline record
    for the smoke flow that creates an object receiving a generated number,
    whose expected result is an identical numbering sequence and format.
    That flow runs through the expression-language library whose version
    advance is the highest-impact runtime fix in the backend track: the
    installed release throws a bytecode verification error from its own
    ASM-based accessor optimizer on the default optimizer path, reproduced even
    against a bean compiled for the older target, which places the defect in
    the library's own generated code rather than in what it reads. The floor
    was established by bisection rather than by documentation - one patch
    release fails and the next succeeds - and the target is the tail of that
    line, 2.4.7.Final advancing to 2.4.15.Final. The consuming surface is what
    makes this load-bearing: there are ZERO rule-language files in the
    repository but 43 live spreadsheet decision tables, and the rule compiler
    declares the expression library with no version of its own, so the
    reactor's property governs which version actually loads. This module owns
    one of those tables, the complaint-number table under its main resources.
    As a web-tier controller test it is equally baseline evidence for the
    preserved REST API contract - paths, HTTP verbs, request and response
    shapes and authentication behaviour are all held constant, and no
    controller signature, request mapping or serialized field changes.
    It runs under the Spring JUnit 4 runner, so it also sits on the Spring
    generation boundary: the framework advances only within its existing 5.3
    line, from 5.3.2 to 5.3.39, and the security modules from 5.4.2 to 5.8.16.
    There is no move to a newer framework generation and no
    application-framework starter.
    The class extends the mocking support base class, so it also exercises the
    mocking library whose version advances from 4.1 to 5.6.0 - the
    widest-footprint test-library change in the migration. Both 4.1 and the
    intermediate 4.3 fail identically on Java 17 with an inaccessible-object
    error raised from that library's class proxy factory; 5.6.0 succeeds, and
    the resolution was to UPGRADE THE LIBRARY rather than relax the JDK module
    boundaries. The same discipline settled the expression-language defect
    above: the library was advanced, never the boundary opened. This file
    consequently carries no module-access launch argument of any kind, and the
    register of JDK internal access exceptions in production launch
    configuration - named here by title only - stays empty on merit rather
    than by omission, because every accessibility failure in this migration
    sat inside a test library and each was fixed at the library.

  WHAT IS ABSENT FROM THIS ARCHIVE, AND WHY THAT PROVES NOTHING
    Six integration-test classes in this module produce no report at all,
    among them the rules-oriented one closest in subject to the numbering
    linkage above. A unit-test run stops at the test phase, and the
    integration-test runner's goals in the root POM are declared with no
    explicit phase, so they bind later. Three further classes declare no test
    method and produce nothing. An absent integration test is NEITHER a pass
    NOR a failure, and it may never justify a test exclusion.

  NOT BYTE-COMPARABLE
